animal  is placing treats on top of the cage or  in front of the cage and seeing if the  animal moves to them we actually had an  investigator it was very creative and  what she did was she put treats in  different corners of the cage and then  she would look the next day to see which  ones were consumed so it gave her an  idea of how mobile the animals were in  the cages you can play streets on top of  the cage to evaluate incisions or wound  healing without actually having to pick  the animals up again this allows  minimizing handling and decreased stress  tree  can stimulate feed intake and rodents  with poor appetite so again it can get  them jump-started also it can just  provide them with an opportunity of  enrichment or foraging opportunity which  we know can decrease stress in animals  it can also act as a distraction from  not feeling well or even from their  incisions these two images just so that  you can give some sort of enrichment for  any situation the animal on the Left who  can't use his paws to pick up a treat  can be given a larger block so that they  can have some form of edible enrichment  and the animal on the right prefers to  take his treats and carry him back to  his shelter on what he liked today  another way to use treats or feed to  minimize stress is to use stress free  oral dosing we know that injections and  handling of the animals do introduce  stress and dr. Lisa bune of the US Army  Research Institute she documented this  this injection stress by measuring core  body temperature and showing the  hypothermia that was introduced when  animals were injected this was  completely eliminated when the animals  were allowed to voluntarily consume the  medication in a treat so medications can  be incorporated into diets or treats in  highly palatable forms that can mask  that our tastes and odors of medications  and again it gives you an opportunity to  minimize stress from handling the  animals at a time when they're there  trying to heal there are a lot of  publications out there which document  the success of using stress free oral  dosing in different formats from  chocolate spreads to peanut butter jelly  is nutritionally complete diets and  documented the animals do consume it  some as quickly as 15 minutes or 30  minutes the drugs for this must be in  oral form you need to be able to monitor  or verify consumption and make sure that  the drug is in an appropriate  formulation and all drugs aren't  palatable so not oral drugs can be used  in this way from our experience we've  seen drugs such as  Plus born can be very difficult to mask  so just to summarize we went over  animals post-operatively are in a hyper  catabolic and hypermetabolic state so  they have an increased need for both  protein and energy they have an  increased protein turnover however the  degradation of proteins exceeding that's  what synthesized so they are in a  negative nitrogen balance things we can  do from the nutritional side is making  sure the animal is in the best state  possible preoperatively making sure the  animals hydrated providing nutritionally  complete diets or treats  post-operatively to get the animal  eating and jump started making sure we  pay attention to the composition and  form of the diet providing animals with  either liquid diets or a softer form and  making it easily accessible for them to  get to and most importantly making sure  that this diet is palatable that's  something that's going to stimulate  their appetite and they're going to want  to eat things we can do from the  enrichment side using edible enrichment  to facilitate either dosing or  monitoring of the animals and really  decreasing stress just by giving them in  a reward we had an investigator that  shared her story in regards to  enrichment she needed to monitor  improvements in dexterity in her rats  after spinal cord injury and she placed  treats in the top of the cage and the  rats had to pull it down and she was  able to monitor help dexterous they were  so after a couple of days of doing this  she found that when she walked into the  room those little paws went up for that  treat so it just shows the power of  rewarding the animals how effective it  can be on your model so next I'm going  to go over some protocols that we've  learned working with other investigators  just to give you an idea of the success  stories that we've heard this first is  just a just a really basic sample  post-operative care plan we always  recommend an acclimation to the recovery  diet at least two to three days prior to  surgery to make sure it's something  that's not novel to the animal at the  time of recovery feeding the  post-operative diet cage level for about  five to seven days providing diets that  are  whether it's a doe a soft diet a gel  diet or liquid diet and we do recommend  providing these fresh daily because they  are soft and we want to keep them that  way and keep them fresh higher moisture  products tend to go bad quicker so  providing it fresh daily is a good idea  it also gives you an idea of how much  the animals are consuming if you change  it out each day you can include a  postoperative analgesia in a treat or a  diet form if appropriate to minimize  handling we always recommend keeping the  standard feed in the hopper and keeping  your standard watering system to give  the animals option if they prefer to eat  that providing basic enrichment in the  cage such as shelter or nesting  opportunity can help decrease stress  levels which is important in recovery  and then it's a good idea to give some  edible enrichment whether giving it for  foraging or a reward or for monitoring  it can really help decrease stress  levels in the rodents this is a protocol  that was shared to us by Nancy defelice  who's been wonderful with her animals  and really creating a wonderful plan to  help her animals recover and have  minimal complications so they do icv  cannulation xin the rats and they  require a four to seven day acclimation  period to whatever they're giving the  animals postoperatively they also  acclimate them to the placebo tablets so  they do use the voluntary oral dosing in  a treat form so the animals are  applicate are acclimated to the placebo  her animals are also given some  enrichment they're given a nylon bone  nesting material and shelters  preoperatively the animals are given  their medicated tablet and some of the  gel diet they'll get postoperatively on  the day of the surgery they're getting  there getting there medicated tablet  they're getting their food cage level  and a gel diet and then they do this  post operatively for 27 days and they  report very few complications when they  have this protocol in place  the next example is from Kim Moscow at  Drexel Kim does a lot of surgeries and  studies on wound healing and she was  having some high mortality and morbidity  rates in this particular model it  involved doing wound checks quite  regularly because that was part of the  study involved putting the animals under  anesthesia to do these rumanceks so she  was seeing a lot of dehydration and loss  of body weight the animals were not  doing well so Kim put together a very  comprehensive plan to turn around her  model so what she did was she acclimated  her animals knowing that the rat sarnia  phobic she started giving a preoperative  analgesia and she also incorporated  nutrition and hydration supplements in  the cage additionally what Kim did to  facilitate these wound check is that she  started a'kla mating the rats to her by  handling them and giving them a reward  after the handling so she was able to  eliminate having to do anesthesia  putting the animals under anesthesia to  do these wound checks and it really  turned around her model she reported  minimized weight loss dehydration and an  overall decreased recovery time this is  one of her very very happy rats this  next example is out of NIH and they had  an undersized knockou